We performed an exhaustive computational search over the domain: p < 50, q < 50, r ∈ [2, 60] (distinct primes p, q), yielding 12,390 valid triples.
For each triple we computed the full residue candidate space R_(p,q,r), applied the three filters (CRT-compatibility, orientation step minimality, orientation uniqueness), and checked whether the resulting generator class contains a small prime representative (defined as a prime <pqr/2┤).
Result: No counterexamples were found.
Only the triple (p, q, r) = (5, 3, 4) produces:
	A unique orientation-minimal compatible residue triple,
	With generator n≡137(mod300),
	Where 137 is the smallest prime in the class.
In all other cases, either:
	No unique minimal residue triple exists, or
	The minimal triple has significantly higher orientation complexity, or
	The smallest prime in the generator class is much larger than pqr/2.
This large-scale scan provides strong numerical support for Conjecture B: the 137-phenomenon is structurally unique to the cycle-length triple (5,3,4) under the Distinguishability Axiom and generative constraints of WGS.

